/**
 * @mainpage CUH - Cloud Utility Hub
 *
 * @section overview Overview
 * CUH simulates incoming requests, a FIFO queue, and a changing pool of web
 * servers. Requests carry input/output IPs, a service duration, and a P/S job
 * type. The program sends no real network traffic.
 *
 * @section design Design
 * cuh::LoadBalancer owns its queue, clock, and servers.
 * cuh::WebServer processes one cuh::Request by counting down its remaining
 * cycles. cuh::Config loads settings, cuh::Firewall checks source-IP ranges,
 * and cuh::Logger writes events and summaries.
 *
 * Each cycle admits new requests, advances existing work, assigns queued work,
 * then checks capacity. New assignments start processing next cycle. More than
 * 80 waiting requests per active server adds one server; fewer than 50 retires
 * one while keeping at least one active. A retiring server finishes its work
 * before deletion. A cooldown separates capacity changes.
 *
 * @section usage Build and run
 * @code{.sh}
 * make
 * ./build/cuh
 * make docs
 * @endcode
 * Enter starting servers and run duration when prompted. For the required run,
 * enter 20 and 100000. Edit config/default.conf to select quiet or verbose mode
 * and adjust traffic, task times, cooldown, blocked ranges, and log path.
 *
 * @section output Logs
 * Quiet mode writes starting and ending summaries. Verbose mode also records
 * arrivals, assignments, completions, blocked requests, and server changes.
 * Summaries report queues, task-time range, work totals, and server status.
 * Accepted requests equal completed plus queued plus in-flight requests.
 * Generated requests equal accepted plus blocked requests, including seeding.
 * The run stops at its selected duration; unfinished requests remain counted.
 *
 * @section bonus Bonus routing
 * bonus_mode=true enables cuh::Switch, which sends P jobs to a processing
 * balancer and S jobs to a streaming balancer. Each owns a separate queue,
 * server pool, cooldown, and statistics. Both advance once per Switch cycle
 * in a single-threaded simulation of concurrent work.
 *
 * Starting servers means total capacity across both pools: 20 starts with 10
 * processing and 10 streaming servers. An odd total gives the processing pool
 * the extra server. Bonus mode requires at least two starting servers and
 * preserves at least one active server per pool. Each initial queue has 100
 * requests per server with that pool's job type. New traffic is generated once
 * and routed by type; each balancer applies the firewall before admission.
 *
 * Logs identify the pool for every event. Summaries report each pool separately
 * and then combined totals. Queue peaks are reported per pool, not summed into
 * a misleading combined peak. Request IDs are scoped by pool; new routed IDs
 * begin above both pools' initial IDs. The configured seed initializes the
 * processing pool; streaming uses seed+1 and shared traffic uses seed+2.
 *
 * Set bonus_mode=false to run the original single-pool simulation.
 */
